{:edb/db-before-t 1622 :edb/db-after-t 1623 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534935 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:27:09.903Z" 13194139534935 true] [17592186087806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320866 13194139534935 true] [17592186087806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320880 13194139534935 true] [17592186087807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320873 13194139534935 true] [17592186088081 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534935 false] [17592186088081 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534935 true] [17592186088081 1100 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}$" 13194139534935 true] [17592186088081 1100 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n1. $y^{\\prime } - xy = 0$\n2. $y^{\\prime } - xy = y$\n3. $y^{\\prime } - xy = x^{3}$" 13194139534935 false] [17592186088081 1145 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534935 true] [17592186088081 1145 "An equation is inhomogeneous when its forcing function is not identically zero after collecting all $y$ terms on the left. Equation I has zero forcing. Equation II becomes $y^{\\prime}-(x+1)y=0$ and also has zero forcing. Equation III has forcing $x^3$, which is not identically zero. Therefore only III is inhomogeneous." 13194139534935 false] [17592186088363 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534935 false] [17592186088363 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534935 true] [17592186088363 1145 "The equation $yy^{\\prime}+x=y$ has a derivative coefficient depending on the unknown $y$. Dividing by $y$, where possible, gives $y^{\\prime}=1-x/y$, which is nonlinear in $y$. The remaining choices can all be rearranged into $y^{\\prime}+P(x)y=Q(x)$; a complicated function of $x$ alone is allowed." 13194139534935 false] [17592186088363 1145 "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{-1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $x{y}^{-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs." 13194139534935 true] [17592186320866 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320867 13194139534935 true] [17592186320866 1089 "q-103551" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320866 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534935 true] [17592186320866 1100 "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320866 1113 #uuid "16833ba9-454a-5aa5-8648-06e8c0ad9832" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320866 1145 "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs." 13194139534935 true] [17592186320867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320868 13194139534935 true] [17592186320867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320869 13194139534935 true] [17592186320867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320870 13194139534935 true] [17592186320867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320871 13194139534935 true] [17592186320867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320872 13194139534935 true] [17592186320867 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534935 true] [17592186320867 1041 "selection" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320867 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320871 13194139534935 true] [17592186320867 1110 #uuid "23eb2ba6-4d1b-5c75-adbb-ad24e70a7072" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320868 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{3}y=\\frac{x}{1+y}" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320868 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534935 true] [17592186320868 1111 #uuid "ee48ef50-7901-5968-8c6e-3ce826fd0abf" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320869 1034 "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320869 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534935 true] [17592186320869 1111 #uuid "56a4decd-d69f-5134-a279-4ab7bfe6d830" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320870 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=x-1" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320870 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534935 true] [17592186320870 1111 #uuid "b27cb28d-67df-56bb-b535-e57e5c99630d" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320871 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320871 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534935 true] [17592186320871 1111 #uuid "3858e614-0306-5bff-bc1c-7bc4762692d7" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320872 1034 "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{3}" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320872 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534935 true] [17592186320872 1111 #uuid "d0219253-1153-52cd-bc9a-45727be4ddec" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320873 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320874 13194139534935 true] [17592186320873 1089 "q-331729" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320873 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534935 true] [17592186320873 1100 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+7xy=5$\n- ${y}^{′}+7xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+7xy=-2y$" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320873 1113 #uuid "6dcfb59a-7071-5f63-8843-aefb72bb7d96" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320873 1145 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=5.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $5$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=-2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+7xy & =-2y \\\\ {y}^{′}+7xy+2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(7x+2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320874 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320875 13194139534935 true] [17592186320874 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320876 13194139534935 true] [17592186320874 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320877 13194139534935 true] [17592186320874 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320878 13194139534935 true] [17592186320874 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320879 13194139534935 true] [17592186320874 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534935 true] [17592186320874 1041 "selection" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320874 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320876 13194139534935 true] [17592186320874 1110 #uuid "11ab446b-775b-5aed-ba93-70a89fadafed" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320875 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320875 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534935 true] [17592186320875 1111 #uuid "92148c48-e4d7-56ae-9599-2cc7bd96aa51" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320876 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320876 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534935 true] [17592186320876 1111 #uuid "45dccf9a-1ae2-5822-ba0f-070623de48a5" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320877 1034 "I only" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320877 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534935 true] [17592186320877 1111 #uuid "5bcadffa-4ca5-51f7-aafe-824a10127836" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320878 1034 "III only" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320878 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534935 true] [17592186320878 1111 #uuid "4ccac42c-b260-5c7a-bd80-38dd2ad4da7b" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320879 1034 "II only" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320879 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534935 true] [17592186320879 1111 #uuid "eb9e0ce4-155c-553c-a397-bde95a14420e" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320880 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320881 13194139534935 true] [17592186320880 1089 "q-79752" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320880 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534935 true] [17592186320880 1100 "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320880 1113 #uuid "ae5b5cf7-dbe2-594e-9c2c-bc9d8807aa4b" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320880 1145 "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $\\sqrt{y}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs." 13194139534935 true] [17592186320881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320882 13194139534935 true] [17592186320881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320883 13194139534935 true] [17592186320881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320884 13194139534935 true] [17592186320881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320885 13194139534935 true] [17592186320881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320886 13194139534935 true] [17592186320881 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534935 true] [17592186320881 1041 "selection" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320881 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320886 13194139534935 true] [17592186320881 1110 #uuid "d81ca39c-dba9-54b9-8984-e3117520499a" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320882 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}={e}^{2x}" 13194139534935 true] [17592186320882 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045